  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Understanding the timeline for kitchen cabinet removal can help you prepare for your renovation. It's not usually a one-and-done kind of job, especially if other trades are involved. Phase 1 is all about preparation. This means clearing out your kitchen completely, disconnecting and shutting off any water and power lines that run to your cabinets or connected appliances. You really don't want any surprises there! Phase 2 involves the actual dismantling. This is when doors, drawers, and hardware are removed, caulk and trim are cut, and the cabinets are unscrewed from the walls. Typically, upper cabinets are taken down before the base cabinets. Phase 3 focuses on the base cabinets. This usually happens after countertops have been removed and any plumbing connections for your sink are safely addressed. Sometimes, this phase needs to coordinate with a plumber. Finally, Phase 4 is cleanup and hauling away the old materials. Keeping this timeline in mind helps you coordinate with other aspects of your remodel. If you need help with the heavy lifting or coordinating with other parts of your project, HH Remodeling LLC is here to ensure a seamless transition."
    }
  ],
